<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Occupation of Japan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as American occupation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was the Allied occupation of the country after its surrender in 1945, directed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It introduced sweeping political, legal and economic reforms, including a new constitution. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> credit these institutional reforms with enabling Japan's rapid post-war economic recovery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan.jpg" alt="Occupation of Japan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scott Alter (Scottalter) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Allied_Occupied_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>American planning for a post-war occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> began as early as February 1942. On 15 August 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Emperor Hirohito</a> announced the surrender in a radio broadcast, having accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration">Potsdam Declaration</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> and the Soviet entry into the war. The occupation was led by the American military, with support from the British Commonwealth and under the supervision of the Far Eastern Commission. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a>, and General Matthew Ridgway succeeded him in 1951. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> took little part. Nearly one million American soldiers served in the occupation over seven years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> never took direct control of civil administration, and the Japanese government continued to operate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>MacArthur's first task was to organise food distribution, since most of the population was starving after the destruction of the cities. Around 5.1 million Japanese returned from abroad in the fifteen months after October 1945. MacArthur kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a> on the throne, arguing that a prosecution would be unpopular, although some Allied leaders wanted him tried as a war criminal. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">SCAP</a>'s insistence the emperor renounced his divinity in a New Year's message.</p><p>Reforms followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s armed forces were disbanded by December 1945, political prisoners were freed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-shinto">State Shinto</a> was abolished and the first trade union law was passed. Of 717,415 wartime officials screened, 201,815 were barred from public office. Women gained the vote, and 39 women won seats in the election of 10 April 1946. Land reform bought about 38 percent of cultivated land from landlords and resold it to those who farmed it. The 1947 constitution, based on a draft by American civilians within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">SCAP</a>, made the emperor a symbol and in Article Nine renounced war. Tribunals such as the International Military Tribunal for the Far East tried war criminals, though MacArthur granted immunity to many, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a> and members of Unit 731.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>From 1947 a Reverse Course shifted American priorities from punishing and reforming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to political stability, economic recovery and, within Article Nine, rearmament in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> aims. Of 325 companies planned for break-up, only the 11 largest zaibatsu were dissolved, and most of those purged returned to public life by 1951. The National Police Reserve, founded in 1950, became the Self-Defense Forces in 1954.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco">Treaty of San Francisco</a>, signed on 8 September 1951, took effect on 28 April 1952, ending the occupation and restoring full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, except for Okinawa and Iwo Jima. Iwo Jima was returned in 1968 and most of Okinawa in 1972. A US-Japan Security Treaty let American troops remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Occupation of Japan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan's postwar recovery, shaped by the <b>American occupation</b>'s institutional reforms, produced the world's second-largest market economy by the late 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>They are attributed to the revolutionary general José Alejandrino, a propagandista, and were written in light of the Philippine–American War and the <b>American occupation</b> that followed, which the song opposed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bayan-ko">Bayan Ko</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic dissolved into the <b>American occupation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/california-republic">California Republic</a></li></ul></section>
